WEBVTT
NOTE why-did-ftx-collapse - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.243
FTX allegedly diverted 8 billion dollars
of customer funds to Alameda Research.

00:00:05.243 --> 00:00:10.424
The Securities and Exchange Commission
details in court filings how these assets

00:00:10.424 --> 00:00:15.605
were routed to an affiliated hedge fund,
creating a massive liquidity shortfall.

00:00:15.605 --> 00:00:21.088
This diversion left FTX unable to satisfy
customer withdrawals when public scrutiny

00:00:21.088 --> 00:00:24.325
triggered a run on the exchange in
November 2022.

00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:28.868
While regulatory filings document the
mechanical steps of this transfer, the

00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:33.769
internal governance failures that enabled
it require examining public records from

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:36.459
federal prosecutors and financial
regulators.

00:00:36.459 --> 00:00:42.182
The collapse of FTX centers on allegations
of commingling customer deposits with

00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:43.184
company funds.

00:00:43.184 --> 00:00:47.943
The Securities and Exchange Commission
filed a complaint alleging that FTX

00:00:47.943 --> 00:00:53.023
executive leadership routinely commingled
customer assets with those of Alameda

00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:55.531
Research, an affiliated trading entity.

00:00:55.531 --> 00:00:59.676
Under basic brokerage and exchange
regulations, customer funds must be

00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:04.296
segregated from operational capital to
protect depositors from trading losses.

00:01:04.296 --> 00:01:09.072
SEC findings indicate that FTX failed to
maintain this separation from its

00:01:09.072 --> 00:01:13.912
inception, allowing customer deposits to
be reallocated without disclosure.

00:01:13.912 --> 00:01:18.899
According to filings by the Department of
Justice, FTX directed customer deposits

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:23.825
into bank accounts controlled by Alameda
Research rather than dedicated exchange

00:01:23.825 --> 00:01:24.872
custody accounts.

00:01:24.872 --> 00:01:29.458
This structural arrangement allowed
Alameda Research to draw directly on

00:01:29.458 --> 00:01:32.835
customer money to meet its own financial
obligations.

00:01:32.835 --> 00:01:37.985
The DOJ indictment explains that Alameda
Research used these diverted deposits to

00:01:37.985 --> 00:01:42.626
fund speculative venture capital
investments, purchase luxury real estate

00:01:42.626 --> 00:01:46.504
in the Bahamas, and make substantial
political contributions.

00:01:46.504 --> 00:01:51.238
As market conditions shifted, these
illiquid assets could not be converted

00:01:51.238 --> 00:01:56.419
back into cash to cover original customer
balances, creating a systemic shortfall

00:01:56.419 --> 00:01:57.699
across the exchange.

00:01:57.699 --> 00:02:02.270
Public court filings from both the SEC and
the Department of Justice detail how

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.899
executive leadership concealed these cash
transfers from auditors and investors.

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:11.847
Financial records submitted to federal
court show that internal balance sheets

00:02:11.847 --> 00:02:16.859
routinely misrepresented the financial
health of both FTX and Alameda Research.

00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:21.473
Depositors operated under the assumption
that their digital assets and fiat

00:02:21.473 --> 00:02:25.595
balances were held safely in isolated
custody accounts, whereas SEC

00:02:25.595 --> 00:02:30.579
documentation confirms those funds were
spent on third-party investments and debt

00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:31.317
settlements.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:35.769
Standard trading accounts on FTX were
governed by an automated risk engine

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:37.573
designed to prevent bad debts.

00:02:37.573 --> 00:02:42.102
The SEC complaint demonstrates that if a
typical user account experienced severe

00:02:42.102 --> 00:02:46.575
losses or insufficient collateral, the
risk engine automatically liquidated the

00:02:46.575 --> 00:02:48.896
user's positions to protect the exchange.

00:02:48.896 --> 00:02:54.270
However, SEC investigators discovered that
Alameda Research was explicitly exempted

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:59.515
from this risk engine mechanism through
customized software logic embedded in the

00:02:59.515 --> 00:03:00.421
FTX code base.

00:03:00.421 --> 00:03:04.397
Federal court filings reveal that
engineers created software features

00:03:04.397 --> 00:03:09.122
allowing Alameda Research to maintain a
negative balance on FTX without triggering

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:09.813
liquidation.

00:03:09.813 --> 00:03:15.390
This exemption granted Alameda Research an
unlimited line of credit drawn directly

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.499
from customer account deposits.

00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:22.972
The Department of Justice documented that
Alameda Research accumulated a negative

00:03:22.972 --> 00:03:28.107
balance running into billions of dollars
while continuing to execute trades.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:33.156
Standard customers received no disclosure
regarding these secret privileges, which

00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:35.557
bypassed all published risk parameters.

00:03:35.557 --> 00:03:40.668
The operational steps of this system
relied on technical modifications to FTX

00:03:40.668 --> 00:03:42.859
internal order matching software.

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:48.390
When a customer initiated a fiat currency
deposit, the software interface instructed

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.419
the bank transfer to land in Alameda
accounts.

00:03:51.419 --> 00:03:55.693
Alameda Research then logged these sums as
internal credits while deploying the

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:57.045
physical funds elsewhere.

00:03:57.045 --> 00:04:01.591
When trading losses accrued at Alameda
Research, the automated system masked the

00:04:01.591 --> 00:04:05.740
deficit by drawing further from the
collective pool of customer deposits,

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.240
compounding the overall financial
shortfall.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.613
A deadline mattered more than a test.

00:04:10.613 --> 00:04:15.936
Nobody had the authority to stop it, or
the person who did had an incentive not

00:04:15.936 --> 00:04:22.115
The financial position of Alameda Research
was heavily reliant on FTT, the native

00:04:22.115 --> 00:04:24.251
utility token issued by FTX.

00:04:24.251 --> 00:04:30.747
SEC filings note that at its peak, the
total market capitalization of FTT reached

00:04:30.747 --> 00:04:34.997
26 billion dollars based on prevailing
market prices.

00:04:34.997 --> 00:04:40.282
Alameda Research held a vast majority of
the circulating and uncirculated FTT

00:04:40.282 --> 00:04:40.763
supply.

00:04:40.763 --> 00:04:45.369
By listing these tokens as high-value
assets on its corporate balance sheet,

00:04:45.369 --> 00:04:50.339
Alameda Research presented an appearance
of immense equity to external lenders and

00:04:50.339 --> 00:04:51.733
financial institutions.

00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:56.723
Court documents filed by the SEC argue
that the 26 billion dollars market

00:04:56.723 --> 00:04:59.525
capitalization was functionally illiquid.

00:04:59.525 --> 00:05:04.253
Because Alameda Research held the vast
majority of FTT tokens, any attempt to

00:05:04.253 --> 00:05:08.981
sell a significant portion on open markets
would cause the price to collapse.

00:05:08.981 --> 00:05:14.421
Nevertheless, Alameda Research used these
artificially valued FTT tokens as primary

00:05:14.421 --> 00:05:19.205
collateral to secure third-party loans
from institutional crypto lenders.

00:05:19.205 --> 00:05:23.853
The DOJ indictment states that executive
leadership actively manipulated FTT

00:05:23.853 --> 00:05:28.745
trading volumes to inflate token prices
whenever collateral values threatened to

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:31.008
fall below loan agreement thresholds.

00:05:31.008 --> 00:05:36.041
This circular leverage model left the
entire corporate ecosystem vulnerable to

00:05:36.041 --> 00:05:36.944
market shocks.

00:05:36.944 --> 00:05:41.841
When third-party lenders accepted FTT as
collateral, they extended cash loans to

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:46.738
Alameda Research, which were then used to
support trading operations and further

00:05:46.738 --> 00:05:47.840
token repurchases.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.665
The SEC complaint highlights that this
created a structural dependency where the

00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:57.489
solvency of Alameda Research depended on
maintaining an artificially high market

00:05:57.489 --> 00:05:58.333
price for FTT.

00:05:58.333 --> 00:06:03.658
If FTT lost value, Alameda Research would
face immediate collateral calls that it

00:06:03.658 --> 00:06:07.141
could not satisfy without liquidating
customer funds.

00:06:07.141 --> 00:06:11.836
The fragile balance sheet model
disintegrated in November 2022 following

00:06:11.836 --> 00:06:15.552
public disclosures regarding Alameda
Research's holdings.

00:06:15.552 --> 00:06:21.272
On November 2, 2022, financial news outlet
CoinDesk published a leaked balance sheet

00:06:21.272 --> 00:06:26.516
showing that Alameda Research held
billions of dollars in FTT against massive

00:06:26.516 --> 00:06:27.333
liabilities.

00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:32.359
On November 6, 2022, competing exchange
Binance announced it would sell its

00:06:32.359 --> 00:06:35.307
remaining FTT holdings due to risk
concerns.

00:06:35.307 --> 00:06:40.495
This announcement triggered a panic among
FTX users, leading to a sudden surge in

00:06:40.495 --> 00:06:41.712
withdrawal demands.

00:06:41.712 --> 00:06:48.693
Over the next 72 hours, FTX faced customer
withdrawal requests totalizing 5 billion

00:06:48.693 --> 00:06:49.365
dollars.

00:06:49.365 --> 00:06:54.077
Court filings in the District of Delaware
bankruptcy proceedings show that FTX held

00:06:54.077 --> 00:06:58.448
only a small fraction of liquid assets
needed to honor these requests, as the

00:06:58.448 --> 00:07:03.046
remainder had been transferred to Alameda
Research or tied up in illiquid venture

00:07:03.046 --> 00:07:03.557
holdings.

00:07:03.557 --> 00:07:08.259
On November 8, 2022, FTX halted all
customer withdrawals.

00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:14.857
On November 11, 2022, FTX and over 100
corporate affiliates filed for Chapter 11

00:07:14.857 --> 00:07:16.672
bankruptcy protection.

00:07:16.672 --> 00:07:21.104
The bankruptcy filings in federal court
revealed that FTX maintained no

00:07:21.104 --> 00:07:26.098
centralized cash management system and
possessed incomplete corporate accounting

00:07:26.098 --> 00:07:26.597
records.

00:07:26.597 --> 00:07:26.997
John J.

00:07:26.997 --> 00:07:31.560
Ray III, appointed as bankruptcy chief
executive officer to clean up the estate,

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:36.238
stated in initial court submissions that
he had never seen such a complete failure

00:07:36.238 --> 00:07:37.493
of corporate controls.

00:07:37.493 --> 00:07:42.356
The bankruptcy process confirmed that the
5 billion dollars withdrawal demand had

00:07:42.356 --> 00:07:46.919
exposed an 8 billion dollars hole in
customer accounts, initiating extensive

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:50.341
recovery litigation by federal bankruptcy
administrators.

00:07:50.341 --> 00:07:55.127
The rapid expansion and prolonged
operation of FTX were facilitated by its

00:07:55.127 --> 00:07:57.003
offshore corporate structure.

00:07:57.003 --> 00:08:02.332
Headquartered in Antigua and later the
Bahamas, FTX operated outside the primary

00:08:02.332 --> 00:08:07.263
regulatory jurisdiction of federal
agencies like the SEC and the Commodity

00:08:07.263 --> 00:08:09.061
Futures Trading Commission.

00:08:09.061 --> 00:08:13.852
Traditional financial brokerages operate
under rigid requirements for capital

00:08:13.852 --> 00:08:17.835
adequacy, third-party custody, and
independent financial audits.

00:08:17.835 --> 00:08:23.037
FTX utilized complex cross-border entity
structures to minimize regulatory

00:08:23.037 --> 00:08:26.341
oversight while serving a global customer
base.

00:08:26.341 --> 00:08:31.009
Federal regulatory filings describe this
corporate arrangement as regulatory

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:31.623
arbitrage.

00:08:31.623 --> 00:08:35.983
By establishing legal entities in
jurisdictions with developing digital

00:08:35.983 --> 00:08:40.405
asset frameworks, FTX bypassed standard
financial reporting obligations.

00:08:40.405 --> 00:08:45.308
SEC and DOJ filings demonstrate that while
FTX US was promoted as an independent

00:08:45.308 --> 00:08:49.720
platform compliant with American
standards, corporate officers routinely

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:54.256
blurred operational lines between US
entities and offshore balance sheets,

00:08:54.256 --> 00:08:58.974
allowing customer funds to be routed
across jurisdictional boundaries without

00:08:58.974 --> 00:09:00.261
regulatory detection.

00:09:00.261 --> 00:09:04.884
The absence of routine regulatory
examinations enabled executive leadership

00:09:04.884 --> 00:09:09.323
to execute complex financial transactions
without independent oversight.

00:09:09.323 --> 00:09:14.203
Reports filed by the SEC and CFTC
following the collapse emphasize that FTX

00:09:14.203 --> 00:09:19.603
lacked an independent board of directors,
established risk committees, or qualified

00:09:19.603 --> 00:09:22.661
chief financial officers across major
entities.

00:09:22.661 --> 00:09:27.114
This lack of basic corporate governance
prevented early intervention by market

00:09:27.114 --> 00:09:31.567
regulators, allowing the alleged fraud to
scale to historic proportions before

00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:34.421
public market forces triggered the final
collapse.

00:09:34.421 --> 00:09:39.416
The edit traces the collapse from internal
software code changes to balance sheet

00:09:39.416 --> 00:09:42.992
inflation, customer runs, and eventual
bankruptcy filings.

00:09:42.992 --> 00:09:47.703
The narrative maintains focus strictly on
documented evidence from federal court

00:09:47.703 --> 00:09:51.413
filings, SEC complaints, and Department of
Justice indictments.

00:09:51.413 --> 00:09:55.796
While federal court filings by the SEC
cite an estimated customer shortfall of 8

00:09:55.796 --> 00:09:59.795
billion dollars with a variance range
around 1 billion dollars, the final

00:09:59.795 --> 00:10:04.123
accounting remains subject to ongoing
bankruptcy liquidations and legal claims.

00:10:04.123 --> 00:10:09.184
The exact total recovered will depend on
asset liquidations in bankruptcy court,

00:10:09.184 --> 00:10:13.865
legal fee deductions, and complex
international claim reconciliations that

00:10:13.865 --> 00:10:15.573
may take years to complete.

00:10:15.573 --> 00:10:20.230
The downfall of FTX demonstrates how
structural governance failures, software

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:24.827
backdoors, and balance sheet manipulation
can collapse a financial exchange.

00:10:24.827 --> 00:10:30.184
Public records from federal court dockets,
the SEC, and the Department of Justice

00:10:30.184 --> 00:10:32.896
document the mechanics of this shortfall.

00:10:32.896 --> 00:10:37.525
This analysis is for educational purposes
and is not financial advice.
